in Hollywood were famous for honoring  those who possess special talents like  acting or directing or singing or  producing but there is one award in our  industry that shines the spotlight on  something more significant something  more meaningful the Jean Hersholt  Humanitarian Award this year's recipient  of the Hersholt is Harry Belafonte  singer producer actor a man whose  talents have entertained millions of  people around the world he has succeeded  in music theater film and television and  he has demonstrated his genius and  brilliance and creativity in all of them  he became a global artist before the  term even existed singing Jamaican  American Jewish and Irish folk songs and  launching the career of South Africa's  Miriam Makeba he's won a Tony Award  Grammys and an Emmy and at the same time  he was doing all of that he has been a  warrior on the good side of the  battlefield of social justice a man  whose true passion for activism has  helped change the lives of tens of  millions of people for the better  Harry Belafonte fought side by side with  dr. Martin Luther King jr. for civil  rights  he helped finance the Freedom Rides in  1961 and the Student Nonviolent  committee Coordinating Committee during  the 1964 Freedom Summer he helped design  the strategies that culminated in the  civil rights legislation of the 1960s  and when dr. King was arrested in  Birmingham Harry Belafonte was a driving  force in ensuring that the Birmingham  movement continued to grow while dr.  King was incarcerated he bailed dr. King  out of City Jail led voter registration  drives at a time when doing that could  result in you being murdered and  he was a leader in the 1963 march on  Washington and that was just what Harry  was doing here at home overseas he  fought against apartheid and apartheid  fell and when he saw that those who  hunger for freedom in Africa had  brothers and sisters across that  continent who hungered for food and  nourishment he came up with the idea for  the we are the world benefit record and  now he's going into the inner cities of  America to help end the plague of gang  violence that affects so many young  people Harry is a voice of reason and  compassion he's a man who has never  wavered once in his lifetime commitment  to his fellow man and women there's a  great line in The Grapes of Wrath from  Henry Fonda's Tom Joad I'll be  everywhere wherever you can look  wherever there's a fight so hungry  people can eat I'll be there wherever  there's a cop beating up a guy I'll be  there I'll be there in the way the guys  yell when they're mad I'll be in the way  kids laugh when they're hungry and they  know suppers ready and when the people  are eating the stuff they raise and  living in the house as they build I'll  be there too Tom Joad was a a classic  but fictional American hero Harry is a  real-life flesh and blood hero a man  who's 87 years on this planet we call  home has been spent fighting injustice  and for human rights and human dignity  Harry's always been on the right side of  history but that has meant that  sometimes he's been on the wrong side of  popular opinion he's the ultimate  example of a great artist and a lifelong  social activist who somehow has done  both better than just about anyone in  either field it's an honor for all of us  to be in the same room with  and for me to present him with this  year's Jean Hersholt Award Humanitarian  Award I love you Harry
 

